.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/is-glob Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "is-glob [![NPM version](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/is-glob.svg?style=flat)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/is-glob) [![NPM monthly downloads](https://img.shields.io/npm/dm/is-glob.svg?style=flat)](https://npmjs.org/package/is-glob) [![NPM total downloads](https://img.shields.io/npm/dt/is-glob.svg?style=flat)](https://npmjs.org/package/is-glob) [![Build Status](https://img.shields.io/github/workflow/status/micromatch/is-glob/dev)](https://github.com/micromatch/is-glob/actions)"
.PP
.RS 4
Returns \fRtrue\fP if the given string looks like a glob pattern or an extglob pattern\&. This makes it easy to create code that only uses external modules like node-glob when necessary, resulting in much faster code execution and initialization time, and a better user experience\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Please consider following this project's author, \fRJon Schlinkert\fP, and consider starring the project to show your :heart: and support\&.
.SH "Install"
.PP
Install with \fRnpm\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install \-\-save is\-glob
.fi
.PP

.PP
You might also be interested in \fRis-valid-glob\fP and \fRhas-glob\fP\&.
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
var isGlob = require('is\-glob');
.fi
.PP
.SS "Default behavior"
\fBTrue\fP

.PP
Patterns that have glob characters or regex patterns will return \fRtrue\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
isGlob('!foo\&.js');
isGlob('*\&.js');
isGlob('**/abc\&.js');
isGlob('abc/*\&.js');
isGlob('abc/(aaa|bbb)\&.js');
isGlob('abc/[a\-z]\&.js');
isGlob('abc/{a,b}\&.js');
//=> true
.fi
.PP

.PP
Extglobs

.PP
.PP
.nf
isGlob('abc/@(a)\&.js');
isGlob('abc/!(a)\&.js');
isGlob('abc/+(a)\&.js');
isGlob('abc/*(a)\&.js');
isGlob('abc/?(a)\&.js');
//=> true
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBFalse\fP

.PP
Escaped globs or extglobs return \fRfalse\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
isGlob('abc/\\\\@(a)\&.js');
isGlob('abc/\\\\!(a)\&.js');
isGlob('abc/\\\\+(a)\&.js');
isGlob('abc/\\\\*(a)\&.js');
isGlob('abc/\\\\?(a)\&.js');
isGlob('\\\\!foo\&.js');
isGlob('\\\\*\&.js');
isGlob('\\\\*\\\\*/abc\&.js');
isGlob('abc/\\\\*\&.js');
isGlob('abc/\\\\(aaa|bbb)\&.js');
isGlob('abc/\\\\[a\-z]\&.js');
isGlob('abc/\\\\{a,b}\&.js');
//=> false
.fi
.PP

.PP
Patterns that do not have glob patterns return \fRfalse\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
isGlob('abc\&.js');
isGlob('abc/def/ghi\&.js');
isGlob('foo\&.js');
isGlob('abc/@\&.js');
isGlob('abc/+\&.js');
isGlob('abc/?\&.js');
isGlob();
isGlob(null);
//=> false
.fi
.PP

.PP
Arrays are also \fRfalse\fP (If you want to check if an array has a glob pattern, use \fRhas-glob\fP):

.PP
.PP
.nf
isGlob(['**/*\&.js']);
isGlob(['foo\&.js']);
//=> false
.fi
.PP
.SS "Option strict"
When \fRoptions\&.strict === false\fP the behavior is less strict in determining if a pattern is a glob\&. Meaning that some patterns that would return \fRfalse\fP may return \fRtrue\fP\&. This is done so that matching libraries like \fRmicromatch\fP have a chance at determining if the pattern is a glob or not\&.

.PP
\fBTrue\fP

.PP
Patterns that have glob characters or regex patterns will return \fRtrue\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
isGlob('!foo\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('*\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('**/abc\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/*\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/(aaa|bbb)\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/[a\-z]\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/{a,b}\&.js', {strict: false});
//=> true
.fi
.PP

.PP
Extglobs

.PP
.PP
.nf
isGlob('abc/@(a)\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/!(a)\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/+(a)\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/*(a)\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/?(a)\&.js', {strict: false});
//=> true
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBFalse\fP

.PP
Escaped globs or extglobs return \fRfalse\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
isGlob('\\\\!foo\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('\\\\*\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('\\\\*\\\\*/abc\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/\\\\*\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/\\\\(aaa|bbb)\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/\\\\[a\-z]\&.js', {strict: false});
isGlob('abc/\\\\{a,b}\&.js', {strict: false});
//=> false
.fi
.PP
